 Beyond the challenge of retrieving complex reasoning traces and temporal data, the successful integration of this multi-faceted information relies entirely on the ``connective tissue'' of the RAG pipeline: query routers and autonomous evaluators. Hashmi \cite{hashmi2026adaptivequeryroutingtierbased} highlighted the rigidity of standard single-channel retrieval, introducing an Adaptive Hybrid Retrieval framework to route queries dynamically, noting that pure vector similarity frequently fails to capture structural navigation. More critically, the evaluation mechanisms governing these routers are themselves open to question. Yalavarthi \cite{yalavarthi2026opensourcereproductionexplainabilityanalysis} conducted a SHAP explainability analysis of the T5-based retrieval evaluator in the widely adopted Corrective RAG (CRAG) architecture, finding that it prioritizes named-entity matching over semantic understanding and shows domain-transfer limitations on scientific questions. The real-world consequences of these shallow heuristics were exposed by Putta et al. \cite{putta2026casefactsbenchmarklegalfactchecking} via the CaseFacts benchmark, which demonstrated that augmenting models with unrestricted web search actively degrades performance in legal fact-checking compared to closed-book baselines. Retrievers that rank by lexical or embedding similarity can return overruled precedents alongside current ones without marking which has been overturned. These findings motivate an explicit entailment check, tested below.
 
